WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 71: The Natchez Trace and the Bandits of the Wilderness Road

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:06.000
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:07.280
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:23.840
[Rebecca] In the late 18th and early 19th centuries, before the arrival of the upstream steam engine, thousands of frontier boatmen floated flatboats loaded with agricultural produce down the Ohio and Mississippi Rivers to the international markets of New Orleans.

4
00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:32.480
[Rebecca] Having sold their crops and wooden boats for hard Spanish silver, these Kintuck boatmen had only one way to return home to Kentucky and Tennessee.

5
00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:38.400
[Rebecca] Walk 500 miles northward through an ancient roadless wilderness trail known as the Natchez Trace.

6
00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:47.360
[Rebecca] Dubbed the Devil's Backbone, this sunken forest road was stocked by America's most cold-blooded highwayman, pirate gangs, and land pirates.

7
00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:54.080
[Rebecca] George brings us the thrilling, dangerous history of the Natchez Trace.

8
00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:58.240
[Rebecca] The Natchez Trace was not an invention of European pioneers.

9
00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:04.400
[George] It was a prehistoric migration corridor worn deep into the North American earth over thousands of years.

10
00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:19.280
[George] Long before human habitation, colossal herds of prehistoric bison and mastodons hammered the path into the soft, wind-blown less soil as they migrated between the mineral salt licks of central Tennessee and the fertile Mississippi River bluffs.

11
00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:32.480
[George] Native American nations, the Chickasaw and Choctaw, developed the trail into a sophisticated network of war paths, diplomatic trade routes, and hunting trails that connected the Cumberland River with the Lower Mississippi.

12
00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:50.720
[George] When French, Spanish, and American traders arrived, they found a corridor unlike any modern road, a narrow, shadowed trench, often barely wide enough for two horses to pass, where continuous foot traffic and heavy erosion had carved the path five to twelve feet deep into the Les Clay.

13
00:01:50.680 --> 00:02:03.480
[George] Flanked on both sides by towering vertical clay bluffs overgrown with ancient magnolias, sweetgums, and wild honeysuckle, the trace felt like a subterranean tunnel through the primeval southern wilderness.

14
00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:18.040
[George] The trail was worn deep into the soft limestone and less bluffs by centuries of seasonal bison migrations, creating shaded sunken corridors where thick cane breaks and towering virgin hardwoods formed a natural green tunnel through the frontier.

15
00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:31.880
[George] The deeply sunken trail, often carved ten to fifteen feet into the soft less bluffs, created natural bottlenecks where visibility was limited to a few yards, providing ideal concealment for lurking predators and highwaymen.

16
00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:39.720
[George] Following the Revolutionary War, the opening of the western territories triggered an agricultural boom across the Ohio River Valley.

17
00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:49.480
[George] In Kentucky, Ohio, and Indiana, pioneer farmers harvested vast surpluses of cured tobacco, smoked ham, corn whiskey, and flour.

18
00:02:49.480 --> 00:03:04.760
[George] Because the Appalachian Mountains were an impassable barrier to freight wagons, farmers built massive wooden flatboats, crude, square boat vessels 70 feet long, and floated downstream along the Ohio and Mississippi Rivers on the spring high water.

19
00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:16.840
[George] Arriving in New Orleans, the flatboatmen sold their cargoes to Spanish and French merchants for stacks of silver atrial pieces, universally known as pieces of eight or Spanish milled dollars.

20
00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:35.400
[George] Because the wooden flatboats could not navigate against the powerful four-knot current of the Mississippi, the boatmen dismantled their craft, sold the lumber, strapped heavy leather money belts weighted with pounds of raw silver around their waists, and prepared for the grueling 500-mile trek northward on foot.

21
00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:46.720
[George] These rough, brawling frontiersmen, universally known along the lower river as Kintucks, were tough as hickory, but their heavy silver made them the most lucrative prey on the American continent.

22
00:03:46.960 --> 00:04:02.480
[George] Flatboat crews celebrated the end of their arduous thousand mile journey down the Ohio and Mississippi rivers by selling their heavy timber boats as scrap lumber, stitching pouches of gold doubloons and Spanish milled dollars into their waistcoats for the long walk home.

23
00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:16.720
[George] Kintucks traveled in loose armed companies for mutual defense, walking twenty to thirty miles a day through dense cane breaks and wading across swollen, alligator infested creeks with their precious silver stitched inside buckskin belts.

24
00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:23.760
[George] The northward journey began at Natchez, Mississippi, a town split between two utterly contrasting worlds.

25
00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:32.320
[George] High atop the two hundred footless bluffs sat Natchez on the hill, an enclave of wealthy cotton planters and fine neoclassical mansions.

26
00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:41.840
[George] But down along the muddy river landing lay Natchez Under the Hill, widely condemned as the most depraved and dangerous sinkhole of iniquity on the American continent.

27
00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:47.920
[George] Packed with rough clapboard saloons, gambling hells, brothels, and counterfeiters' dens.

28
00:04:47.920 --> 00:05:00.160
[George] Natchez Under the Hill was a chaotic den of thieves where unscrupulous gamblers, swamp outlaws, and river pirates waited to swindle, drug or murder boatmen before they even set foot on the trace.

29
00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:14.720
[George] Those boatmen who survived the vices of Under the Hill banded together in armed companies of a dozen or more, checking their flintlock pistols and heavy hunting knives as they entered the dark, 400 mile wilderness of the Choctaw and Chickasaw nations.

30
00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:29.280
[George] Natchez Under the Hill was notorious along the Mississippi as a cesspool of gambling dens, brothels, and waterfront taverns, where ruthless river pirates and card sharps preyed on drunken boatmen celebrating their successful cargo runs.

31
00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:41.560
[George] Waterfront saloons in Natchez under the hill buzzed with fiddle music and brawling river men, while lookouts in the employ of highway gangs quietly noted which boatmen were carrying heavy purses of Spanish gold.

32
00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:50.760
[George] Among the most monstrous outlaws who stalked the trace were Mikedja and Wily Harp, universally known to frontier lore as Big Harp and Little Harp.

33
00:05:50.760 --> 00:06:03.800
[George] Originally from North Carolina, the Harp brothers were murderous Tory renegades who waged an unhinged, psychopathic campaign of terror across Kentucky, Tennessee, and Mississippi in the late 1790s.

34
00:06:03.800 --> 00:06:13.880
[George] Unlike ordinary highwaymen who robbed for financial gain, the Harps murdered with sadistic, gratuitous pleasure, killing lone travelers, homestead families.

35
00:06:13.880 --> 00:06:24.200
[George] And even young infants, disemboweling their victims, filling the abdominal cavities with heavy stones, and sinking their corpses in river beds to conceal their crimes.

36
00:06:24.200 --> 00:06:29.080
[George] In 1799, an enraged Kentucky posse tracked the outlaws.

37
00:06:29.080 --> 00:06:41.160
[George] Cornering Big Harp near modern-day Lapers Fork, posse member Moses Stegall, whose wife and infant son had been butchered by the bandit, shot Big Harp and severed his head with a hunting knife.

38
00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:46.600
[George] Placing the skull in the fork of an oak tree at a crossroads known to this day as Harp's Head.

39
00:06:46.600 --> 00:06:52.040
[George] Little Harp escaped, fleeing south onto the Natchez Trace to join other bandit gangs.

40
00:06:52.040 --> 00:07:06.760
[George] Big Harp and Little Harp claimed dozens of victims along the Wilderness Road and the Trace, leaving a trail of mutilated bodies that horrified frontier settlements and prompted territorial governors to offer massive bounties for their captured dead or alive.

41
00:07:06.760 --> 00:07:19.280
[George] Frontier passes pursued the bloodthirsty Harp brothers across multiple states, enduring weeks of tracking through trackless Appalachian ravines and swamps before finally cornering the sadistic killers in western Kentucky.

42
00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:24.800
[George] The undisputed mastermind of highway robbery on the Natchez Trace was Samuel Mason.

43
00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:33.840
[George] Unlike the psychotic Harps, Mason was an intelligent, suave former Revolutionary War officer and magistrate who turned to organized crime.

44
00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:45.360
[George] Operating from Caveenrock on the Ohio River before moving south to the swamps of the Natchez Trace, Mason organized an elaborate syndicate of road agents, horse thieves and spies.

45
00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:53.200
[George] Mason positioned clean-shaven informants in Natchez taverns to observe which boatmen carried the heaviest leather pouches of Spanish silver.

46
00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:59.840
[George] When a targeted company departed, Mason's scouts marked their progress with notched blazes on trail trees.

47
00:07:59.840 --> 00:08:12.880
[George] Mason's gang ambushed the travelers in narrow, sunken cuts where escape was impossible, robbing them of silver and leaving his mocking signature carved on a tree, done by the hand of Samuel Mason of the woods.

48
00:08:12.880 --> 00:08:22.000
[George] For years, territorial governors in Mississippi and Orleans territories offered massive bounties for Mason's capture, dead or alive.

49
00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:36.880
[George] Mason established fortified hideouts along the rocky bluffs of Caven Rock on the Ohio River and the remote Cypress swamps of the Trace, directing a disciplined gang that ambushed pack trains and murdered wealthy travelers with methodical brutality.

50
00:08:36.880 --> 00:08:49.760
[George] Mason was an educated revolutionary war veteran whose polished manners and courteous demeanor deceived unsuspecting travelers, allowing him to lead victims into isolated ravines before his cutthroat gang attacked.

51
00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:54.960
[George] Mason's reign ended in 1903 in a sensational act of frontier treachery.

52
00:08:54.960 --> 00:09:05.720
[George] Little Harp, operating under the alias John Seton, had joined Mason's gang, lured by the $2,000 state reward posted by Mississippi Territorial Governor William C.

53
00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:06.040
[George] C.

54
00:09:06.440 --> 00:09:19.880
[George] Claiborne, Little Harp and an accomplice waited until Mason was asleep in a swamp hideout, shot their chieftain through the head, severed his head, and encased it in a heavy ball of blue river clay to preserve it from decay.

55
00:09:19.880 --> 00:09:27.560
[George] The two outlaws rode into Old Greenville, Mississippi, presenting the ball of clay to the circuit judge to claim the reward.

56
00:09:27.560 --> 00:09:34.120
[George] As the clay was washed away in a water bucket, local citizens recognized the features of the notorious bandit.

57
00:09:34.120 --> 00:09:44.440
[George] But just as the reward was about to be counted, a passing traveler in the courtroom looked at the claimant and shouted, That man is not an honest citizen, he is Little Harp.

58
00:09:44.440 --> 00:09:50.760
[George] A former possiman stepped forward, examined the scar on Harp's chest, and confirmed his identity.

59
00:09:50.760 --> 00:09:55.560
[George] The outlaws were arrested, convicted of murder, and hanged on the gallows.

60
00:09:55.560 --> 00:10:01.880
[George] Their severed heads were impaled on wooden posts along the Natchez Trace as a gruesome warning to highwaymen.

61
00:10:01.880 --> 00:10:15.720
[George] Territorial officials were so determined to confirm the bandit leader's demise that they placed Mason's head on public display atop a wooden pole along the trace near Greenville, Mississippi, as a gruesome warning to all frontier highwayman.

62
00:10:15.720 --> 00:10:27.400
[George] The dramatic betrayal of Mason by his own lieutenant Wiley Harp resulted in both outlaws meeting their fate on the gallows, ending the reign of terror that had haunted the wilderness road for a generation.

63
00:10:27.400 --> 00:10:32.520
[George] The era of the Natchez Trace outlaws ended almost overnight in 1811.

64
00:10:32.520 --> 00:10:47.120
[George] In December of that year, the New Orleans, the first steam-powered vessel to navigate the western waters, successfully completed its voyage from Pittsburgh to New Orleans, proving that steam could overcome the powerful current of the Mississippi.

65
00:10:47.120 --> 00:10:53.120
[George] Within a few short years, flatboatmen no longer had to walk 500 miles through the wilderness.

66
00:10:53.120 --> 00:11:00.560
[George] They bought passenger tickets on luxury paddlewheel steamboats, returning to Kentucky in six days in comfort and safety.

67
00:11:00.560 --> 00:11:06.320
[George] The sunken road fell into silent disuse, reclaimed by the wild southern forest.

68
00:11:06.320 --> 00:11:09.760
[George] In the 1930s, President Franklin D.

69
00:11:09.840 --> 00:11:21.120
[George] Roosevelt's New Deal established the Natchez Trace Parkway, creating a 444-mile scenic national parkway that follows the historic route from Natchez to Nashville.

70
00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:36.560
[George] Today, modern travelers can walk along preserved sections of the sunken trace beneath ancient, moss-draped oaks, feeling the deep hush of the earth where bison, Chickasaw warriors, and brave Kintucks once walked the devil's backbone into American history.

71
00:11:36.560 --> 00:11:53.520
[George] The advent of the commercial paddlewheel steamboat allowed travelers to journey upriver in comfort, rendering the overland trail obsolete until the 1930s, when Congress transformed the historic corridor into the 444-mile scenic Natchez Trace Parkway.

72
00:11:53.520 --> 00:12:08.320
[George] Today, modern travelers along the parkway can stop to walk preserved sunken sections of the old trace, experiencing the quiet, ancient forest canopy that once echoed with the shouts of pioneer flatboatmen and frontier outlaws.

73
00:12:16.560 --> 00:12:23.920
[Rebecca] Join us next week as we journey to the historic French Quarter of New Orleans for the story of the Louisiana Purchase.

